data.content:
Here's what changed and why:

**Approach:** Use .NET 8's built-in [`TimeProvider`](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.timeprovider) — zero new interfaces, zero DI setup.

**What I did:**
- Constructor takes an **optional** `TimeProvider?` (defaults to `TimeProvider.System`), so existing callers are unaffected.
- Replaced `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow` with `_clock.GetUtcNow()`.

**In your tests**, add the `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet package, then:

```csharp
var fakeClock = new FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));

var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fakeClock);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

fakeClock.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(30)); // move time forward mid-test
```

Apply the same pattern to any other class that reads `UtcNow`.
